# Both spellings on purpose. A trailing slash matches directories ONLY, and git records a
# symlink as a file, so a `dist` symlink into another worktree is NOT covered by `dist/` and
# `git add -A` stages it with an absolute path baked in. That is a broken checkout for
# everyone else plus a leak of one machine's layout, from a habit as ordinary as pointing a
# branch at an already built tree.
node_modules/
node_modules
dist/
dist
# The release staging directory. release.yml creates it, and scripts/build-verifier.sh writes
# binaries into whatever directory it is given, which is this one during a release and during any
# local rehearsal of it. Ignoring it is not tidiness: neither `dist/` nor `dist` above covers it,
# because a gitignore pattern matches whole path components and `dist-release` is not `dist`. So a
# `git add -A` after a local dry run commits five cross-compiled binaries, an npm tarball, and an
# SBOM. That happened while building this, which is why the rule is here.
#
# Both spellings, for the symlink reason given at the top of this file.
/dist-release/
/dist-release
.env
.env.local
*.log
coverage/
.DS_Store

# Python bytecode and caches, from verifier-py. Compiled per interpreter version, so
# committing them would put one machine's CPython minor into everyone's checkout.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# An operator's live config and policy. Anchored to the repository root with a leading
# slash: a bare `policy.yaml` matches at ANY depth, which silently swallowed the shipped
# examples/policy.yaml. It survived only because it was tracked before the rule existed,
# so the breakage was invisible here and would have appeared for anyone building the tree
# without git history.
/agentwall-approvals.json
/agentwall.config.local.yaml
/agentwall.config.yaml
/agentwall.config.yaml.init-default
/agentwall.lan.yaml
/policy.yaml
/state/
/.codex/

# Editor and rescue copies. A backup of an ignored file is not itself ignored by the
# rule that ignores the original, so an operator's live config can reach a public repo
# through `git add -A` the moment someone snapshots it before an edit.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig
*~
*.swp


# Local isolated implementation worktrees.
/.worktrees/